module lib/hf/jk
  The Coulomb and exchange matrices J and K: the two-electron part of the Fock
  matrix, built by contracting the electron repulsion integrals with a
  density. `J_ij = sum_kl (ij|kl) D_kl` is the repulsion from the whole
  charge cloud; `K_ik = sum_jl (ij|kl) D_jl` is exchange, the same integrals
  with the indices crossed. A closed shell uses `F = H + J - K/2`.

  Three in-core members read the integrals stored once per 8-fold symmetry
  class (lib/hf/eri_store) and must agree
  [test "the three members agree to 1e-12 on water 6-31G (one density) and OH (two)"]:
  a single pass over the packed vector (production), a pair matrix, and the
  full four-index tensor used literally. lib/hf/integrals adds a direct
  member that computes integrals on the fly.

jk_packed  @daf34e47
  why    It reads each stored integral once and needs no search for the other
         index orders it stands for, so it is the cheapest of the three in time
         and memory.
  how    The loops visit the unique quadruples $i \ge j$, $k \le i$, and $l \le k$
         (or $l \le j$ when $k = i$) in the order eri_store packs them. Each
         stored value stands for up to eight index orders; halving it once for
         each coincidence ($i = j$, $k = l$, the pair $ij$ equal to $kl$)
         leaves one unit of weight per distinct order, so adding it in all
         eight orders counts every term once.
  X1     the packed integrals $(ij|kl)$, one per symmetry class, in eri_store's order
  X2     not read: the slot a direct member uses for shells
  X3     not read: the slot a direct member uses for primitives
  D      the densities, `[nd, n, n]`: one for a closed shell, one per spin otherwise
  opts   the options column array (lib/hf/opts); not read
  sh     the shape of D
  nd     how many densities
  n      the number of basis functions
  R      the result, `[2 nd, n, n]`: J of density d at `2 d`, K at `2 d + 1`
  d      the density being contracted
  Dd     density d, `[n, n]`
  Ds     `Dd + Dd^T`: the density on a pair in both orders, which every Coulomb term takes
  J      the Coulomb matrix of density d, filed at `2 d` in R
  K      the exchange matrix of density d, filed at `2 d + 1` in R
  p      the position of the next stored integral in X1
  lmax   the bound on l that keeps the quadruple in the packed order
  g      the integral being added, after its weight is corrected
  dkl    g times the density on the pair kl, both orders: its Coulomb term on ij
  dij    g times the density on the pair ij, both orders: its Coulomb term on kl
  i      the first index of the stored quadruple $(ij|kl)$
  j      the second index, $j \le i$
  k      the third index, $k \le i$
  l      the fourth index, below `lmax`

  = g      read the next stored integral; the three halvings below correct its weight
  = dkl    the Coulomb term goes to $J_{ij}$ and $J_{ji}$, and the next line's to $J_{kl}$ and $J_{lk}$
  = K#2    exchange: the eight index orders, each pairing one index of ij with one of kl

jk_pairs  @30a8b587
  why    The pair matrix makes J one matrix-vector product per density, which
         is a second way to get J with different arithmetic than the packed
         pass; the agreement test compares them.
  X1     the packed integrals, read here as the lower triangle of the pair matrix
  X2     not read
  X3     not read
  D      the densities, `[nd, n, n]`
  opts   not read
  sh     the shape of D
  nd     how many densities
  n      the number of basis functions
  np     the number of index pairs $i \ge j$, `n (n + 1) / 2`
  P      the pair index of each $(i, j)$, `[n, n]`, symmetric
  G      the integrals as a symmetric pair matrix, `[np, np]`
  a      a pair index: the row of G
  b      a pair index: the column of G
  v      one stored integral
  R      the result, `[2 nd, n, n]`: J at `2 d`, K at `2 d + 1`
  dv     the density folded onto pairs: `D_kl + D_lk` off the diagonal, `D_kk` on it
  d      the density being contracted
  Jv     J of density d on pairs: `G dv`
  s      the sum being built for one entry of K
  i      a row index of J and K
  j      a column index of J; in K's sum, the index summed with `l`
  k      the column index of K; in the folding, the first index of a density entry
  l      the second index of a density entry, summed in K

  = dv     fold the density onto pairs, so each pair's two orders are counted once
  = Jv     J: G times the folded density, one entry per pair
  = s#2    K: the crossed contraction, reading G through the pair table

jk_dense  @3ad625ba
  why    It applies the two definitions exactly as written, on the full tensor,
         so it is the member a reader can check by eye; the others must agree
         with it.
  X1     the packed integrals, unpacked here to the full tensor
  X2     not read
  X3     not read
  D      the densities, `[nd, n, n]`
  opts   not read
  sh     the shape of D
  nd     how many densities
  n      the number of basis functions
  G      every integral $(ij|kl)$, `[n, n, n, n]`
  R      the result, `[2 nd, n, n]`
  d      the density being contracted
  Dd     density d, `[n, n]`
  i      a row of J and K
  j      a column of J and K

jk_methods  @ee12ecd0
  why    The SCF picks its J/K builder by number (`OPT_JK`, through `jk_of`);
         the registry lets a test sweep every in-core member on the same
         input.

jk_of  @c8c74154
  why    The SCF loop calls one def whichever builder the options pick, so the
         loop does not know which members exist. It calls each member by
         name, the packed one when the option is left at its default, so the
         SCF reaches J and K without a registry.
  Gp     the integrals, passed in all three slots of the member signature
  D      the densities, `[ndens, n, n]`
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_JK]` picks the member
  k      the member the options name: `JK_PACKED`, `JK_PAIRS`, `JK_DENSE`,
         `INT_JK_DIRECT` or `INT_JK_DIRECT_FULL`

jk_update  @27a8e4e7
  why    J and K are linear in the density, so the build from the change of
         density since the last build, added to the last J and K, is the
         whole build up to rounding and to what screening drops. The direct
         member's density screening skips the quartets that the change does
         not reach, so a direct SCF that builds this way does less work as it
         converges; PySCF's direct SCF builds its potential from
         `dm - dm_last` the same way.
  Gp     the integrals, or the basis blob for a direct member
  D      the densities now, `[ndens, n, n]`
  Dlast  the densities J and K were last built from
  JKlast the J and K built from them
  opts   the options column array

jk_rebuild  @a2aa33e4
  why    The build from the whole density, the last one unused: what the
         incremental build is held to, and what the SCF does every
         `fock_rebuild` iterations and once it is close to converged.
  Gp     the integrals, or the basis blob for a direct member
  D      the densities, `[ndens, n, n]`
  Dlast  not read
  JKlast not read
  opts   the options column array

jk_build_methods  @50773dde
  why    The two ways to build the next J and K side by side, for the test
         that holds them together.

jk_t_opts  @166a40ee
  why    The tests pick members by number; this builds the options array for
         each, with the other options at their defaults.
  k      the member number

jk_rhf_energy  @b16f6a3b
  why    The members must agree with each other and also give PySCF's energy
         from PySCF's density
         [test "PySCF's densities give PySCF's energies through every member"].
  Gp     the packed integrals
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian, kinetic energy plus nuclear attraction, `[n, n]`
  D2     PySCF's converged total density, `[n, n]`
  enuc   the nuclear repulsion energy
  k      the member number
  D      D2 as a stack of one density
  JK     J and K of that density

jk_uhf_energy  @05f40b82
  why    The same check with two spin densities, where exchange acts within
         each spin only.
  Gp     the packed integrals
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian, `[n, n]`
  Da     PySCF's alpha (spin-up) density
  Db     PySCF's beta (spin-down) density
  enuc   the nuclear repulsion energy
  k      the member number
  D      the two spin densities stacked
  JK     J and K of each spin density: `J_a, K_a, J_b, K_b`
